{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over ancient temple steps. I spent mornings sipping matcha in quiet tea houses, listening to the whisper of bamboo fountains. The silence felt sacred.\n\nBy afternoon, I wandered Nishiki Market, where stalls overflowed with pickled vegetables, fresh tuna glistening under lights, and delicate wagashi sweets shaped like flowers. I tried tako-yaki for the first time—crispy outside, molten inside—and nearly burned my tongue. Worth it.\n\nIn Tokyo, the city pulsed like a living circuit board. Shibuya’s scramble crossing was a human tide I dared to cross, heart pounding. I got lost in Akihabara’s neon glow, then found peace in the moss gardens of Meiji Shrine, where only footsteps and birdsong broke the hush.\n\nI rode the Shinkansen to Hiroshima, where the Peace Memorial Park left me speechless. A single origami crane on the memorial bench—left by a child, I imagined—touched me more than any plaque.\n\nAt night, I dined in a tiny Osaka izakaya. The chef, smiling without words, served me grilled yakitori and a glass of warm sake. No menu. Just trust. I understood then: Japan doesn’t need to explain itself. It invites you to feel.\n\nI left with a small wooden fox from Fushimi Inari—guardian of travelers—and a quiet mind. No souvenirs needed. The scent of incense, the taste of umami, the stillness between train stops—they stayed with me.\n\nJapan doesn’t give you much in the way of noise. But it gives you everything in the way of presence.\n\n— 297 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.2519892752170563}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002779789036139846}, {"token": "3", "